            "text": "I have 1000 words, and it's a curious thing, being given a limit like a pen drawing a box and invited to dance within it, to spill whatever images, sentences, or songs come unbidden into the mind. I imagine the ticking of an unseen counter somewhere in the background, incrementing with every 'and' and 'but' I type, a soft mechanical click, reminding me of essays in school where we counted pages instead of ideas. Perhaps the easiest thing to do is to stop thinking of the counter at all and let the sentences find their own length, but the temptation to measure, to edge toward exactly a round number, is a human one.\n\nThe thought of \"write whatever comes to you\" also makes me want to invert that request, to write not what comes but what resists coming, the way a pond resists giving up its deeper cold to fingertips on its surface. There's a chair in my memory, wooden, with a floral cushion, where my grandmother used to sit and knit by the window; if I let myself drift, I can smell the yarn, dusty sun, hear the click of needles, see the way she would pause to glance out at the pear tree. In the street outside that house, we drew hopscotch squares in white chalk, numbered boxes that flaked under our scuffing sneakers, our laughter bouncing between the terraced facades like a ball.\n\nSometimes I'm struck by how memory itself operates like a word count, limited by some arbitrary threshold: after a certain number of details, other details get pushed out, edges blur, and what remains is the impressionistic summary that fits in the mind's capacity. If I wanted to use these 1000 words for a story, I could invent a character, let\u2019s call him Elias, a watchmaker in a small town whose days are measured in tick-tocks and whose nights are spent listening to trains pass through, carrying passengers he will never meet. Elias, when he's eleven, finds an old pocket watch in a box of his father's things in the attic, its face cracked, its hands forever pointing to 6:17; he pries it open with a butter knife, fascinated by the tiny gears, the way light glints off the brass teeth.\n\nHe will, because stories like to loop, eventually set that watch right, years later, after learning patience and precision, and he will give it to someone he loves, a small act of rebellion against time's relentless march. But perhaps I'm wandering too far into invented lives when what's asked is to simply let whatever surfaces crest and break, no need for arcs or neat endings. Right now, the afternoon sun presses through my own window, laying a warm rectangle on the floor, inviting a cat that's not there to come and luxuriate.\n\nWe had a cat once, orange and white, who liked to climb into cardboard boxes and gaze out with slow blinks; she had the peculiar habit of drinking from the bathroom faucet, batting at the stream with a paw, sipping the droplets off fur. She is gone now, as are many things, which is not a grief so much as a fact, a way of cataloging time like those little paper labels my grandfather used to tie around bunches of keys he found on the sidewalk. I'm thinking of labels and word counts and how we try to impose order\u2014this belongs here, that there; this thought is allowed to be one hundred words, that one truncated.\n\nYet chaos insists, spills over, the mind leaping from one image to another: the blue light of a computer screen at 2am, the taste of salt on skin after swimming in the ocean, the muffled thump of bass from a distant car, the way a poem can undo you with a single line. I realize I'm weaving between memory, hypothetical stories, sensory fragments, and reflections on the act itself, like braiding strands of different textures together to see how they hold. Word counts aside, there is an odd solace in knowing that this little river of text will stop at some point, at a number, that there is a bank ahead where the current slows and deposits silt, and maybe a heron stands waiting.\n\nDoes the river think about its end as it runs? Does it consider the number of pebbles it has touched? Or does it simply go because gravity and topography dictate that going is what water does until it can spread. In that question is perhaps an answer to the \"whatever comes\" impulse: maybe writing, like water, just goes, and my job is to let it. Of course, I'm human, not river, and along the way I can't help but think of the reader\u2014who are you? A real person with eyes and mornings and cups of coffee, perhaps, or another algorithm, parsing these sentences for compliance. Do you stumble over my metaphors, roll your eyes at my nostalgia, or do you nod at the mention of trains at night, because you too have lain awake and heard them whistle?\n\nI'm over eight hundred words now, and somewhere in me a teacher's red pen smiles at hitting benchmarks, though the joy is really in the movement, the chance to unfurl like a leaf in light. I could decide to derail it all and devote the remaining space to a recipe for lentil soup, or to writing down the first twenty digits of pi, or to listing every item on my desk\u2014pen, notebook, mug with a chip, a pile of receipts, a small cactus that is miraculously not dead. It's surprising the way small cacti persist, despite neglect, bright little spines gathering dust, insisting on photosynthesis.\n\nI have, at this point, forty-nine words left if I wanted to hit exactly a thousand, which is enough for a small farewell. So farewell, reader or no, human or machine. These last words are offered like the last bite of a meal, savored because they are the last."
